[model] lru_cache_ttl: A comprehensive, thread-safe LRU cache with Time-To-Live (TTL) expiry.
[model] 
[model] This module provides a robust caching mechanism suitable for high-performance applications
[model] that require both Least Recently Used (LRU) eviction and time-based expiration.
[model] It is designed to be thread-safe, allowing safe concurrent access from multiple threads.
[model] 
[model] Key Features:
[model] - LRU eviction policy: Removes the least recently used item when capacity is exceeded.
[model] - TTL expiry: Items expire after a specified duration, regardless of usage.
[model] - Thread safety: All operations are protected against race conditions.
[model] - Configurable capacity: Set a maximum number of items to keep in the cache.
[model] - Flexible TTL: Each entry can have a different TTL or a global default TTL.
[model] - Metrics and statistics: Track cache hit/miss rates, eviction counts, etc.
[model] - Customizable decorators: Use the cache as a decorator for function arguments.
[model] - Async support: Includes an async version for use with asyncio.
[model] 
[model] Usage Examples:
[model]     # Basic usage with decorator
[model]     @lru_cache_ttl(maxsize=128, ttl_seconds=3600)
[model]     def expensive_computation(key):
[model]         time.sleep(0.1)
[model]         return key * 2
[model] 
[model]     # Manual usage
[model]     cache = lru_cache_ttl(maxsize=100, default_ttl=60)
[model]     result = cache.get("key_1")
[model]     cache.evict("key_2")
[model] 
[model]     # Async usage
[model]     async def async_call():
[model]         cached = await lru_cache_ttl_async(maxsize=50)(async_func)("key")
[model] 
[model] Performance Considerations:
[model] - The implementation uses a combination of a doubly-linked list (for LRU ordering)
[model]   and a hash map (for O(1) lookups).
[model] - TTL checks are performed lazily during get/put operations to minimize overhead.
[model] - Thread safety is achieved using internal locks and atomic operations where possible.
[model] 
